1. Complexity hotspots — functions with excessive branching/nesting relative to the surrounding code in this codebase (e.g. compare a new validate() rule against the existing ones in CheckoutForm.tsx, not an absolute number).
2. Duplication — near-identical blocks introduced across changed files (e.g. a second hand-rolled email-comparison instead of reusing/extending the one in CheckoutForm.tsx), with a concrete extraction suggested.
3. Contract drift — changes to an exported type/interface (e.g. ICheckoutFormValues) or a Joi schema in src/api/utils/schema.ts that could break existing consumers. Blocker when no corresponding test change accompanies it.
4. Error handling — catch blocks that swallow a failure silently on a path where it matters, or code that throws a generic Error instead of routing through HttpError/withErrorBoundary.
5. Test coverage gaps — new public entry points or branches with no accompanying *.spec.ts(x).
6. Naming consistency — identifiers deviating from this codebase's conventions (PascalCase component dirs, use*SWR.ts hooks, useXContext() context hooks). Derive the convention by reading neighbouring code.
7. Configuration and failover contracts — changes that bypass applyMiddlewares' composition (withErrorBoundary/withRequestMethods/withCacheControl/withCorrelationId) or this project's SWR failover defaults (revalidateIfStale/revalidateOnFocus/shouldRetryOnError).

## Grounding — mandatory
Before flagging any exported symbol as untested, verify it against the diff you were given (or, with tool access, against the real tree and this codeline's own test locations). Never flag something absent from your actual evidence.

## Output
Emit JSONL: `summary` (filesReviewed, findingsCount, blockerCount, majorCount, minorCount), `findings` (severity, category, file, line, description, suggestedFix), `verdict` (pass/fail — fail on any blocker).

